<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Start Monday. | Canada2080"
    description="Greg Hart on potential, living systems, and relentless pragmatism, from his cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Capacity · 1:55 p.m."
    headline="Start Monday."
    name="Greg Hart"
    bio="Greg Hart is co-founder and facilitator at InceptionU, founder of Future Fit Cities, and a member of the TransPod Advisory Board. His work connects systems thinking and strategy to practical implementation."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/greg-hart-design/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5417.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Greg Hart speaking beside the podium. His name is on the slide."
    photos={photos}
>
    <p class="lede">
        2080 is not a problem to be solved. Know where you are going, know where
        you are, and start Monday.
    </p>
    <p>
        He works closer in than 2080, and this question is the centre of that
        work. Easy problems should be solved. The challenge of the country's
        potential is different. If someone calls it a wicked problem, his reply
        is that it is not really a problem. It has to be thought about another
        way.
    </p>
    <p>
        His picture was a boat loaded with containers of beliefs and
        assumptions. The future is a process. It never arrives, and a static
        point in it is dangerous. VUCA, volatility, uncertainty, complexity,
        and ambiguity, has been around for twenty years. He said it describes
        the future, today, and the past. We feel it more now.
    </p>
    <p>
        The same thinking produces the same results. He cited Sidney Dekker on
        the drift into failure: you wake up and it has already gone, and it
        took a long time to get there. The danger is not only missing something
        remarkable by 2080. It is arriving at something remarkably bad.
    </p>
    <p>
        He borrowed Warren Buffett, riffing on Socrates and Buckminster Fuller.
        If the boat is leaking, look for a new one. Keep fixing the holes you
        have. Also find a new paradigm. There are no broken systems. A system
        produces the patterns it produces. If you dislike them, rework it from
        a new way of seeing. That is strategy, not a map, and potential, not a
        problem list. The question is what could be created.
    </p>
    <p>
        Design, as he defined it, is creating the conditions for success. You
        have to know what success looks like, and you have to know the soil
        things will grow in. People say Canada does not move fast enough. He
        agrees. The current Prime Minister wants major projects approved
        quickly. Speed, he said, is not the design goal. It is what low
        friction produces. Friction means the paradigm is poor.
    </p>
    <p>
        Apollo, for him, was a mission built on potential, not a narrow answer
        to Sputnik. Many technologies in use now came from it. He offered it as
        a way government can shape a market around potential.
    </p>
    <p>
        The paradigm he wants, including from an Indigenous perspective, is a
        living system, because life creates the conditions for more life.
        Living systems run on potential and relationships. They do not choose
        quantity over quality, or the highest energy option. They keep what
        works and still invest in what would be different. A living system has
        to be competent. Competence is know-how.
    </p>
    <p>
        If more of the world's people are coming, as he heard Francis suggest,
        the places they live in matter. Utrecht built an expressway into the
        middle of town in 1980 and later retrofitted it. Places can be made
        better. Ignore what you are putting together, he said, and the result
        is a Blade Runner future.
    </p>
    <p>
        Education, in his view, is outdated for this work. Rewire it around
        critical thinking, systems thinking, and computational thinking, not
        facts to be returned on a test. Make it experimental, and a form of
        play.
    </p>
    <p>Four things that work has to know.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>What patterns do we want?</li>
        <li>What conditions do those patterns need?</li>
        <li>What actions support them?</li>
        <li>How do we measure that we are on the track?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        He called that relentless pragmatism. Know where you want to go. Know
        where you are, without romance. Know what it takes to get from here to
        there. If the horizon is 2080, start Monday.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Hey everybody. Thanks for inviting me, Francis. It is great to
                be here.
            </p>
            <p>
                2080 feels like a long way away, so you'll see me talk a little
                bit more about things that are closer. All the things that I'm
                involved in are all related in some way really to this topic.
                This is the centre of the work that I do every day, all day.
            </p>
            <p>
                And so I'd like to talk to you today about a few basic ideas
                for how we might think about this going forward, starting with
                this idea of: we should just stop solving problems. Of course,
                there are some problems that are easy to solve, and we should
                solve them. But a lot of the challenges, the challenge we're
                talking about today, is not a problem-oriented challenge. What
                we do to create the potential of this country for 2080, some
                people would call it a wicked problem. My response would be: if
                it's a wicked problem, it's not really a problem. It's
                something we have to think about in a completely different way.
            </p>
            <p>
                So, there we go. I took this picture myself. No, I didn't. But
                this is a picture of the future. That's what this is. It's a
                picture of the future, and we can add to the future us. That's
                us right there at the bottom in the boat filled with
                containers. Those containers are filled with all sorts of
                beliefs and assumptions that we have about how the world works
                and how we work in that world.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is the situation that we find ourselves in. You know,
                there are all sorts of acronyms that explain this in different
                ways. This is a popular one. It's been around for 20 years or
                more now: the notion of VUCA. These things are all true about
                the future, they're all true about today, and they're also all
                true about the past. We're just feeling the pain of this a lot
                more today because of a lot of the changes that have occurred.
            </p>
            <p>
                And remember, the future is a process. It never arrives. So if
                we think about a static point in the future, it can be a little
                bit dangerous.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here's another thing to think about when we think about all
                those containers that are on that ship: the same old thinking
                leads to the same old results. It's what my friend Sidney
                Dekker in Australia calls the drift into failure, where all of
                a sudden you wake up one day and everything's gone to shit, and
                you're wondering what happened, and it took a long time to get
                there. We are in danger of that situation right now, let alone
                achieving something remarkable by 2080. Actually, avoiding
                something remarkable and negative by 2080.
            </p>
            <p>
                Warren Buffett is somebody you've probably heard of, and I love
                this. He's basically riffing on Socrates and Buckminster Fuller
                here by saying, hey, you know what? If you're in a boat that's
                leaking and things are not going well, you should spend some
                time looking for a new boat. Which isn't to say that we stop
                fixing the holes in the boat that we have for the moment, which
                is problem-solving, but we need to look for a new boat. That
                means a new paradigm, a new way of looking at the world.
            </p>
            <p>
                And remember, there are no broken systems. This is language we
                hear all the time: oh, the system's broken. It's not. It's just
                producing patterns you don't like. If that's the case, then
                think about how the system can be reworked from a new paradigm.
                It isn't about setting out plans. It's not about maps to the
                future. It's about strategy. It's not about problems. It's
                about potential, thinking about what could we create?
            </p>
            <p>
                In order to do that, we have to think about context. For me,
                design is really a simple thing: it's creating the conditions
                for success. Design requires us to know what success looks
                like, and it requires us to understand the conditions that will
                bring about that success. That's a long way from a narrow,
                problem-solving kind of approach.
            </p>
            <p>
                Which means that we need to think about the context. What is
                the soil that we are creating in order for things to grow in?
                For instance, people say things don't happen fast enough in
                this country. I completely agree they don't happen fast enough.
                The current Prime Minister is quite concerned about getting
                major projects approved quickly. But speed itself is not
                actually a design goal. Speed itself is a byproduct of good
                design. If things are put together in the right way and the
                right kind of potential is being created, things will naturally
                go fast with low friction. If there's lots of friction, it
                means the basic paradigm is poor.
            </p>
            <p>
                I put this slide here to show you that it's possible for a
                bunch of middle-aged or older white guys to get into a room and
                something cool could happen. I'm referring here to the Apollo
                missions from the 1960s. The Apollo mission was a
                potential-based mission. It was not a problem-solving mission.
                You could say it was about solving the problem of the Russians
                getting Sputnik into space, but that would be a very narrow
                definition. Many of the technologies we use today came from
                this mission. I would say that it's really interesting to think
                about this as a way of shaping a market. When government thinks
                about how to participate, shaping markets around potential is a
                huge potential for them.
            </p>
            <p>
                The paradigm we need to connect with, or reconnect with if we
                think about the Indigenous perspective, is a living systems
                perspective, because life creates the conditions for more life.
                That's what we're talking about in this kind of world. Living
                systems are based on potential and relationships. They never
                choose quantity over quality. They never look for the highest
                energy option. They always look for the lowest energy option.
                They're interested in options. They want to maintain and
                operate the things that are working today, but they also invest
                energy in the future, in things that are different than today,
                what new value would look like. This is an important paradigm
                that we could think about.
            </p>
            <p>
                One of the most important things coming out of a living system
                is it has to be competent, because if it's not competent, it's
                not a living system. Competence is fundamental, and that's
                know-how.
            </p>
            <p>
                If we're going to deal with a future like this, we need to
                learn how to surf. There are two things I want to highlight
                here. One is the places that we live, because this is the
                context for humanity. If we're talking about half the world
                moving to Canada as Francis seems to be suggesting by 2080, the
                places we live are important. This is a picture of Utrecht in
                the Netherlands in 1980 where they built this horrible
                expressway into the middle of town, and they've retrofitted it
                back to the picture you see on the right. It's possible to
                improve the quality of places, using living systems principles
                in order to create better places to live that are more
                successful so that we don't end up in a Blade Runner future,
                because Blade Runner is what happens when you don't pay
                attention to what you're putting together.
            </p>
            <p>
                Learning is a huge challenge. Our education system right now is
                completely outdated for the challenges that we're talking
                about. It has to be completely rewired around fundamentals like
                critical thinking, systems thinking, and computational
                thinking, not bits and pieces of facts and factoids that get
                thrown at us and that we have to regurgitate onto tests. We
                should become very experimental, and it should be a form of
                play, something that we celebrate. These are the conditions
                that will lead to this kind of prosperity that we're looking
                for. That means we have to know: what are the patterns we want?
                What are the conditions that are needed? What are the actions
                that support those patterns? How do we measure all of that
                stuff to make sure that we're on the right track?
            </p>
            <p>
                I would sum this up by saying this is a principle of relentless
                pragmatism. It means knowing where you want to go, having a
                clear idea of where we are today, and not being a romantic
                about it, but actually knowing what's needed to get from here
                to there. This is something that's missing, and we need to
                bring it to bear really quickly.
            </p>
            <p>So if we're talking about 2080, I'm saying start Monday.</p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
