                <h3 id="panel">Panel</h3>
                <p class="transcript-note">Francis Wang asked the questions.</p>
                <p class="ask">
                    How do we know we are making progress as a country? What
                    signals, in ten years or sooner, would show we are not just
                    running isolated projects?
                </p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        I'll take a crack at it. I'm actually involved in a
                        project right now that is very focused on measurement
                        around these kinds of questions. Measurement is the
                        key. You have to be able to see what's actually
                        happening with regard to the key principles that you
                        know are going to put us in a position to realize
                        potential.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        First, we have to know what we're playing for. I think
                        for a long time we have not had any idea about that.
                        It's all just catch-up, and we've got to be as good as
                        these guys, or how do we hold on to this? It's very
                        much this problem-solving behaviour. So first, we have
                        to know what it is. Second, we have to make it visible.
                        Everybody should be able to look around and see: hey,
                        those things we said are important, are they working or
                        not? Are we doing them or not? Currently, we're just
                        not doing the things that need to be done. So for me,
                        that's a big part.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        And we can actually measure how often we do that. That
                        would be important to talk about.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Barry makes such a great point, and I want to tie it to
                        something said earlier about the bear going into
                        hibernation or coming out of hibernation. To me, when
                        the bear really went into hibernation was when we
                        signed the Free Trade Agreement with the United States
                        in the 1980s. It cemented a relationship of absolute
                        comfort and shifted our percentage of sales to one
                        country from 40 or 50 percent to well over 80 percent.
                        It was the thing that allowed us to go to sleep. So
                        hopefully we have woken up, and hopefully we're going
                        to be hungry coming out of the den and do some stuff.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <p class="ask">
                    What about Monday? What are we heading for first?
                </p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        When I used that phrase start Monday, the most
                        important thing is not to push off a timeline, but to
                        actually start. The thing to start on Monday from where
                        we are right now is to build the compass. We have to
                        say: this is the place that we're going, and this is
                        what it requires of us to get there. Then, immediately
                        start doing the little things at whatever scale
                        necessary so that the compass needle tracks to the
                        place we want to go. Know what the principles and
                        conditions are, and know how to start putting those
                        into play immediately.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        That way, when Sebastien fills out the request for
                        proposals for that project in Alberta, instead of
                        having to worry about bureaucrats figuring out how not
                        to say yes to something exciting, or using a technology
                        that works well elsewhere but won't work in this
                        situation, we've created the right conditions. Right
                        now, we're nowhere close to that. So when I say start
                        Monday, it means we know what needs to exist to get to
                        the future we want, so let me start living like that on
                        Monday. Even at an individual level, we can start
                        approaching our day with the mindset and practices
                        necessary to achieve the things we want.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Yeah, we'll check on the bear. We already have a
                        measurement suggested for us right now.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
